.TH BC 1
.SH NAME
bc \- arbitrary-precision calculator language, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bc
[
.B -e
.I script
]
[
.B -f
.I scriptfile
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bc
is a Limbo-native workalike for a bounded subset of
.IR bc (1),
the calculator language, compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in. It is an infix front end over the same
exact fixed-point arithmetic
.IR dc (1)
already implements in this tree \(em see BUGS for the one real limit
that arithmetic has.
.PP
.I Bc
reads a program from
.I file
arguments (concatenated in order), or standard input if none are given,
or from
.B -e
.I script
and
.B -f
.I scriptfile
arguments (repeatable, evaluated before any files). Ordinary text is a
sequence of statements, separated by newlines or
.BR \&; .
A bare expression statement prints its value followed by a newline; an
assignment (including a compound one, below) does not print anything by
itself.
.PP
Numbers are digits with an optional
.B .
and more digits (a leading or trailing
.B .
alone is fine, e.g.
.BR .5 );
a leading
.B \-
is the unary negation operator, not part of the literal. Variable and
function names are one or more letters, digits and underscores, starting
with a letter or underscore.
.SS Operators
.B "+ - * / %"
and
.B ^
(power, integer exponent only, positive or negative) work as in
.IR dc (1);
unary
.B \-
binds
.I tighter
than
.BR ^ ,
so
.B \-2^2
is
.RI ( \-2 )^2
= 4, not
.BR \-(2^2) .
Comparisons
.B "== != < > <= >="
and logical
.B "&& || !"
yield 1 or 0.
.B =
.RB ( "+= -= *= /= %= ^=" ", too)"
assigns, right-associative, and the assignment itself has a value (so
.B "x = (y = 5) + 1"
works) \(em but see BUGS for the one corner this does not replicate.
Postfix
.B ++
and
.B \-\-
return the old value, then adjust the variable (prefix
.B ++/\-\-
are not supported, see BUGS).
.SS Control flow
.BI if( cond ")" " stmt-or-block"
[
.B else
.IR stmt-or-block ],
.BI while( cond ")" " stmt-or-block" ,
and
.BI for( init ; cond ; incr ")" " stmt-or-block"
work as in C, where a
.I stmt-or-block
is either one statement or a
.B { ... }
block; any of
.IR init ,
.IR cond ,
.I incr
may be empty (an empty
.I cond
is always true).
.SS Functions
.BI define( name ", " params ")" " { " stmts " }"
defines
.I name
to run
.I stmts
when called as
.BI name( args ")" ,
returning the value of a
.B return
.RI ( expr )
statement (a bare
.B return
yields 0; falling off the end of the function also yields 0).
.B auto
.IR name ", " name ", " \&.\|.\|.
inside the body declares local variables: each named
.I name
(and each parameter) shadows any global of the same name for the
duration of this call only, restored to whatever it was \(em or removed
entirely, if it did not exist \(em when the call returns. There are no
separate local scopes beyond this: a function can call itself (or
another function) recursively, and each call gets its own shadowed
bindings.
.SS Builtins
.TP
.BI sqrt( x )
Square root, truncated to the current scale.
.TP
.BI length( x )
The number of decimal digits in
.IR x 's
mantissa, ignoring its scale entirely \(em
.B length(0.001)
is
.BR 1 ,
not 4.
.TP
.BI scale( x )
.IR x 's
own number of fraction digits (not the current scale register).
.TP
.B scale
Used
.B without
parentheses, the current scale register itself \(em an ordinary
variable to read or assign
.RB ( "scale = 4" ),
except that assigning to it changes how
.BR / ", " % ", " ^ ", and " sqrt
round, exactly like
.IR dc (1)'s
.BR k .
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/bc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bctest (1),
.IR dc (1)
.SH BUGS
This is a deliberately bounded subset. No
.BR ibase / obase
(input/output radix \(em always base 10), no
.B \-l
(the math library:
.BR s / c / a / l / e / j ),
no string literals or
.B print
statement, no arrays, no
.BR pushdef / popdef -style
definition stacks beyond the single
.B auto
scope above, no
.BR read() ,
no prefix
.BR ++/\-\- .
.PP
One real grammar corner is deliberately not replicated: in the real
oracle, a bare (unparenthesized) assignment whose right-hand side
touches a relational operator parses with assignment binding
.I tighter
than the relational operator \(em
.B "y = 3 > 2"
is
.RI ( "y = 3" )" > 2" ,
leaving
.B y
at 3 and printing the comparison's result
.RB ( 1 ),
not what it looks like at a glance. This parser instead requires
parentheses around a relational subexpression used as a value
.RB ( "y = (3 > 2)" ,
setting
.B y
to 1) \(em a real POSIX-bc oddity, checked against the host and then
deliberately scoped out rather than replicated, since matching it
exactly needs a second, separate expression grammar for relational
operators alone.
.PP
Numbers are exact only up to a real limit: Limbo's
.B big
is a 64-bit signed integer here, not arbitrary precision, despite this
program's name \(em see
.IR dc (1)'s
BUGS for the full correction and the measured threshold (matches the
host through roughly 18\(en19 significant decimal digits, then this
program fails with
.B "precision exceeded"
rather than silently printing a wrapped-around wrong answer).
.BR bctest (1)
checks the exact boundary
.RB ( sqrt(2)
at scale 9 still matches, scale 10 now fails loudly, where it used to
print a nonsense negative number).
.PP
Every rule above (bare-expression printing, unary-minus-before-power
precedence,
.B length
counting mantissa digits only,
.B auto
shadowing rather than a fresh scope) was checked against the host's own
.I bc
(Gavin Howard's bc 7.x) with targeted probes before writing any of
this, the same discipline as
.IR dc (1)/ m4 (1)/ awk (1)
\(em not read off a bc manual from memory, since most such manuals
describe the older AT&T/GNU bc, not this one.
